/* iOS permission usage descriptions — English (developmentRegion).

   These four strings are the ONLY place iOS lets us answer "why does this app
   want that?" before the user decides. Each one answers exactly that and
   nothing else: no imperative telling the user what to tap, and no vague
   "to improve your experience" — a permission line the user cannot map onto a
   real capability is the same failure shape as a status word that cannot say
   what it is based on.

   The values here duplicate the defaults in Info.plist on purpose: Info.plist
   is what ships when the device locale matches no .lproj, and iOS reads THIS
   file when the locale is English. Both must say the same thing. */

"NSMicrophoneUsageDescription" = "FlowMic uses the microphone to turn what you say into text on your PC.";

"NSCameraUsageDescription" = "FlowMic uses the camera to scan the pairing QR code shown on your PC.";

"NSPhotoLibraryUsageDescription" = "FlowMic reads the photo you pick so it can send that picture to your PC.";

/* Local network: the LAN channel dials the PC directly on the same network.
   Saying so plainly is the point — this is the one permission whose purpose a
   user has no other way to guess. */
"NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription" = "FlowMic connects directly to your PC over the local network, so what you say reaches it without going through the internet.";
